INT. HOLLOWAY KITCHEN - NIGHT

The power has been out for eleven minutes, and in that time Hannah Voss has built a small, ordinary world out of candles and tea lights, the way she once built forts out of couch cushions, certain that if the room looks cozy enough then nothing outside it can be wrong.

Dark. Candle flames. The fridge, silent.

Desmond, her husband, 40s, stands at the window with his phone held up like a lantern, and he is not looking at the sky. He is looking at the street. Every house on it is dark in exactly the same way.

Rosalind, 16, sits on the counter swinging her heels. Beside the stove, Ilya, Hannah's father, 70s, hums something tuneless and kind. Nothing wrong.

Beck, the neighbour, 50s, leans in the back doorway with a flashlight pointed politely at the floor.

HANNAH
Grid's down to the coast, Beck says. Probably a transformer.

BECK
Probably. Probably a transformer.

THE TWINS
(together, from under the table)
Can we have the marshmallows now?

ROSALIND
You had marshmallows.

THE TWINS
That was before.

ILYA
Before what? Before what?

DESMOND
(not turning)
Hannah. Come here.

HANNAH
In a second, Des, the girls are...

DESMOND
Now.

She crosses the kitchen. Wax. Tea. The smell of home.

Outside, above the roofline, the stars are wrong. Too many. Too still. And one of them is moving toward the house.

Silence.

Her phone. Dead.

BECK
That is not a transformer.

ROSALIND
Mom?

THE TWINS
Is it the visitors?

Nobody breathes. Hannah turns, slowly, and sees that Ilya has stopped humming, and that her father is smiling at the window like a man whose train has finally come in.

ILYA
(softly)
They said it would be in the evening.
